 @jibberjabber6919  electricity costs are all over the place around the world (even between neighboring states) so it's hard to pick. And what is "long term"? 3 years? 5 years? more than that?
And how much hours of daily gaming is representative? And what kind of games? All of those parameters are quite tricky to chose.
Though the main issue with power draw is more about in-case thermals and how much the system overheat a room rather than the bill itself.

@Hardwareunboxed This is marketing scheme to push sales of the more expensive products. 
Buy item at X or pay 10% more to get up to 25% more performance. 
WOW, all of a sudden going for the more expensive card makes more sense, and the company makes more money from a sale..

 @WyattOShea  Unfortunately, not much. I have 2 second-hand cards (kinda repaired) to say I've overclocked SLI.
Neither of my cards even goes above 2000MHz. But, they never ever power throttle anymore, so hold maximum boost forever of 1950MHz  & 1935MHz respectively. 
Cost is 300-320W (compared to 250W per Nvidia spec). Next step would be volt-mods. Which I know doesn't do much on Pascal.
